    "result_text": "There's only one test class, and the full suite is green. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI added a derived `timezone` field to the owner response, following the same pattern the codebase already uses for `locality`.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`Localities.java`** \u2014 Added a `REGION_TIMEZONE` table (`NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney`, `VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne`, `QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane`) and a `timezoneForRegion(region)` lookup returning `null` for unknown regions.\n\n2. **`CustomerCodes.java`** \u2014 Added `timezoneOf(owner)`, which derives the timezone from the owner's existing `localityOf(owner)` region, mirroring how `locality` is computed.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added a MapStruct `@Mapping` for `timezone` using `CustomerCodes.timezoneOf(owner)`, alongside the existing `locality` mapping.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added the read-only `timezone` string field to the owner schema (the `OwnerDto` is generated from this spec), documenting the fixed region-to-timezone table.\n\nThe `timezone` derives from the same locality/region already computed for the owner, so a `Sydney`/postcode `2000` owner resolves to `NSW` \u2192 `Australia/Sydney`. Build succeeds and the test (`coreReturnsTimezoneForRegion`) passes.",
